[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059ba794-5e1e-5457-bdea-b64dbf8af255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250598} {:answer/id #uuid "10b69a39-7e8c-5f74-896a-d443bd7304be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250697} {:answer/id #uuid "11354cf1-977d-5955-8b92-e5f5eb109c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250702} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b5fbcf-da28-5414-abe5-919e7481946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252162} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e20126-30f4-5509-99b2-6d09012d8ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b69a39-7e8c-5f74-896a-d443bd7304be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250697} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f0404d-47dd-51ab-b2d1-37abcadbb9fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d27a8b9-840b-5e81-b68e-56b475bcaf90" :question/math-academy-id "q-116798" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-5)+4,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-5)+4 \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (0-5)+4 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{1}{3})≈2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca01609-0a8b-5ee1-a86e-9ea04ed6fe2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251741} {:answer/id #uuid "a8615bcd-5800-5dd9-ba76-7facffd6dc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252010} {:answer/id #uuid "b96a0caa-65dd-5577-be12-ca3294e512b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252184} {:answer/id #uuid "c40f88e6-74ad-5084-bfce-8992e7981c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252291} {:answer/id #uuid "de30bdd3-59c6-5f53-9fd6-3692956c6922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8615bcd-5800-5dd9-ba76-7facffd6dc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252010} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb629470-c03e-5a0f-8d5d-fca92c2a3682" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2cb32bd7-bce1-5088-9a7e-4c63d6e35647" :question/math-academy-id "q-116800" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(2)={e}^{2}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{2}-2=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | ${e}^{2}+2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{5}{2})≈{e}^{2}+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dfb223-94da-59c9-93d7-40747b254902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250895} {:answer/id #uuid "632efb00-5e1f-5eeb-87a6-be90c76ce2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251364} {:answer/id #uuid "965b4e6d-0b10-5173-a0ba-bc2dedcc6a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251843} {:answer/id #uuid "9d818da9-724c-5b6d-b0fc-dc9670a79bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251914} {:answer/id #uuid "f084042b-4913-5aa5-b6ed-ff51f6655714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27dfb223-94da-59c9-93d7-40747b254902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250895} :answer-field/id #uuid "a390b7c9-522f-5c15-b279-5cf16154b3ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1718b71d-74bc-5780-811c-5db2e873c658" :question/math-academy-id "q-116886" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(-1)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+(-3) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fd5935-a6d2-5b39-acf7-bb6003859673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250700} {:answer/id #uuid "63f04560-efb0-5870-a4e2-32593e791830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251371} {:answer/id #uuid "de5ef8ec-701c-5c7c-b90e-4ebe987fc175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252511} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7886ed-0b78-565a-a9c1-1ea94ef119b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252641} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1e21c1-72e7-550f-b287-010bb2b39415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f04560-efb0-5870-a4e2-32593e791830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251371} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af55769-4924-594b-ba0e-ed85228b1c66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd07643b-ca8f-5e00-9ad5-265bd5d31225" :question/math-academy-id "q-117408" :question/problem "$2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62398511-9191-54bf-9678-afe8b77b6df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251351} {:answer/id #uuid "76e51c07-ae30-5f76-9211-c622eb4edaba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251534} {:answer/id #uuid "7db2cbba-40a7-517d-85d4-4db5c32721c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251608} {:answer/id #uuid "c42fdbb0-808e-537e-ae57-9e0a4f2d9b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252292} {:answer/id #uuid "e30522a4-eaa9-513c-932a-1eb516b48504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62398511-9191-54bf-9678-afe8b77b6df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251351} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e46c9e-0f1c-54e1-8afd-4cf347c19516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a5d4f00-fa86-576c-9cad-eafafdb18fe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-117411" :question/problem "$-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4506a2a0-4a66-51aa-a003-d9f643a2f7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251125} {:answer/id #uuid "646e1641-365e-53ec-bf58-e219647ee54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251375} {:answer/id #uuid "6897262f-9c63-5884-a06a-50434981336a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251411} {:answer/id #uuid "93a9ff23-0972-5e3c-9993-18fea9dd977c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251821} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d322b5-3e3c-5f03-9243-09ddd91e5e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d322b5-3e3c-5f03-9243-09ddd91e5e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252186} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a35cff-79b6-5d48-8d7a-ed6977457c32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fdb4740-bcc2-5647-bec0-b3bf354a5523" :question/math-academy-id "q-117412" :question/problem "$10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e104022-bb4e-565d-a6bf-bd962d901ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251070} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7c7f6e-d175-5ced-b466-3c36c4f17838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251317} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ea3bf9-1407-5df8-8f30-0caede0decbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252322} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e6f228-8f8a-57cb-9af2-282ce558e2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252406} {:answer/id #uuid "eef4021b-6b37-5e54-b681-f27585eee147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7c7f6e-d175-5ced-b466-3c36c4f17838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251317} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48b917e-69fa-56ff-b770-34926780baa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b40d2d1-a796-5057-96c3-9515dd6af44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117442" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3{x}^{3}+24$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+24 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{3} & =-24 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-2\\in [-3,-1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n![](images/80/80315b4862adf8ebb1f91f5c4c7606a59dfa5738c40e34179a745e03a0a25693.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-3,-2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot ([4-16]-[\\frac{81}{4}-24]) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-12+\\frac{15}{4}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{33}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{99}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,-1],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot ([\\frac{1}{4}-8]-[4-16]) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{31}{4}+12) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{17}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{99}{4}+\\frac{51}{4}=\\frac{75}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57317c5d-547c-5676-9794-216eb7820aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251266} {:answer/id #uuid "5e49eea4-11c0-55ab-a94f-bf0ce604cfa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251331} {:answer/id #uuid "629a7e52-8f4e-5bed-bb8c-c8b4074dbe61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251355} {:answer/id #uuid "b9119585-69d4-5571-a90b-11589d386ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252182} {:answer/id #uuid "c66a43b9-c276-5c36-80e8-c1ebb31d2aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9119585-69d4-5571-a90b-11589d386ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252182} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b32753c-b724-5f6f-aa1e-07ed9f98d5ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9f3c4b9-a238-51ae-b5c9-d1395c8e83c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117457" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85660ae9-4f2e-5621-a014-ab47c7110fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251671} {:answer/id #uuid "862e8743-0a18-5e52-9f70-2ff15d02a428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251678} {:answer/id #uuid "ae14b8c7-3fd3-53d4-beb7-4b473c691e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252070} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c548d1-7499-5fd9-b385-aaaac7b68c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252098} {:answer/id #uuid "cd04858f-5250-51d1-ade7-ddf22507a99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd04858f-5250-51d1-ade7-ddf22507a99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252361} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8709c71-c949-55ae-bbde-e386757bf140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ae088f2-83ab-519c-8adf-de49960f46a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-117459" :question/problem "$3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =3\\cdot ({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091dfe6d-dbcd-5dc8-908f-031833bf5679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250636} {:answer/id #uuid "37d5087b-6859-5f3e-8798-9a0961309a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251014} {:answer/id #uuid "690726ad-749f-5890-8274-26648cc3d209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251420} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e69f0d-76ca-5918-8497-cea75026a9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252108} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c9e48f-2c4b-51db-b2d5-abde59af8121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d5087b-6859-5f3e-8798-9a0961309a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251014} :answer-field/id #uuid "938dd839-cb93-5473-921d-1230977f6699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "faf88325-d673-5e64-a0a2-e5898606cfcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-117461" :question/problem "$-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) & =(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ecc819-acb1-5df5-9748-8605bfca9317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251015} {:answer/id #uuid "68a0f002-cae4-5c37-8a69-4b724f88ce62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251413} {:answer/id #uuid "81c81ed5-e80c-5c65-a8b3-082014a382f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251648} {:answer/id #uuid "990be92e-936a-5c07-9036-ee48d86256d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251868} {:answer/id #uuid "ce85ac4a-bc9b-5c3a-8299-d9d1ea40fe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68a0f002-cae4-5c37-8a69-4b724f88ce62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251413} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac16ee52-49d1-5b8b-9170-ffd9df8262f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24e984a2-585a-5608-b3a2-5f94b69b88d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-117462" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05263274-8a30-5b9e-99c8-bc4632800973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{2},6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250593} {:answer/id #uuid "26c93fc6-e7c9-527e-b808-8e8ae2deae84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250886} {:answer/id #uuid "622a263a-6ba2-5b98-88d5-099953d3f597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251350} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5a5357-7eb2-5b78-bbad-edc691df5fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251758} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9086da-236a-59d4-9d03-b14bbfe51a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd9086da-236a-59d4-9d03-b14bbfe51a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252777} :answer-field/id #uuid "49dfe913-70a3-528c-b8ca-29e7f976f6c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8647f1c-5367-5a91-bc69-e78441ce9412" :question/math-academy-id "q-117463" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(6,7)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-12x-14(\\frac{7}{2})+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79b4f2fe-61fe-595e-9afc-66fee93d0af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251565} {:answer/id #uuid "99d6d289-0be0-5b5b-a332-7709c8440710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251875} {:answer/id #uuid "a110bdeb-4b4c-5c86-a664-7f5138d2c1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251943} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5205e0-26dd-5e9a-ba86-f903d4f49d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252050} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1632aa-3461-5d5c-a789-47a490ec4b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a110bdeb-4b4c-5c86-a664-7f5138d2c1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251943} :answer-field/id #uuid "01ac0480-1bc1-5114-8725-a56fa87f797d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52486dc2-a5ad-5323-887e-ec059a62d92e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11753" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(8,4),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 16 & =32p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133fede6-e6b3-5934-9ab6-600b897e76c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250719} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb9ca10-8ea3-5cda-814e-af155fd838bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251583} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c06d53-55cc-51d9-bc54-73807258d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252328} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c2b286-9473-5923-a0df-50419f5281e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252465} {:answer/id #uuid "ef673879-992e-531a-9cc6-362a14d4f889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bb9ca10-8ea3-5cda-814e-af155fd838bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251583} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1d9cb5-e489-5d90-befc-482609c96a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b52b18c-f18d-51d2-9931-b8f3ebe75944" :question/math-academy-id "q-11756" :question/problem "![](images/58/58c92cc542f027ec882191d2b4631f9fcf3704820b2825a18f30dbdc3f59d07b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-4,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,2),$ we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 4 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a66e0c-95d3-55b9-b3ba-7a5a5e78709a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250992} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd4c776-c0be-5732-a407-f7edbca1a409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251066} {:answer/id #uuid "57e53e6d-b266-517e-ad73-38153bdd8c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251270} {:answer/id #uuid "aabb06ee-3ceb-5738-9f76-0e5f376bcff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252032} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb24956-8a43-55e6-a408-c64426c8f93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aabb06ee-3ceb-5738-9f76-0e5f376bcff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252032} :answer-field/id #uuid "45857773-a925-5062-b5dc-38ca097e9df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ad97703-d400-5d43-94f9-d8eae5764f59" :question/math-academy-id "q-11757" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-6,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-6,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4161544d-5503-509c-a919-47b75546d693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251094} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0b8f2d-8bf9-5548-96dd-fe899f5d298f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252036} {:answer/id #uuid "ba23df8f-a679-5d5c-bde8-2b7238d24224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252191} {:answer/id #uuid "c9834705-9dcd-5c24-afb6-f095959807d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252335} {:answer/id #uuid "f25fe0b5-3a2d-5c1e-b5a3-425fbf7a30c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9834705-9dcd-5c24-afb6-f095959807d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252335} :answer-field/id #uuid "918ffa31-2155-56a2-9928-59d9d880148e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53685d44-d788-5019-bc63-97c441bd2453" :question/math-academy-id "q-117578" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{3}{4})}=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71fbeca3-4760-5d6a-856c-a573ea317eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{300}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251486} {:answer/id #uuid "9229fa93-9ffb-5473-9b2e-ff4e454a6679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251803} {:answer/id #uuid "ca888844-ac33-5b63-a641-8bce7947f0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{480}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252347} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fdabb1-db44-594d-8452-62e77cc9289f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252685} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9b6765-ab75-54b3-a097-f8617bfd508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9229fa93-9ffb-5473-9b2e-ff4e454a6679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251803} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf475618-9da6-5ade-89c7-309940393d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0d5cec2-04d5-542c-903e-28cdf3f5b313" :question/math-academy-id "q-117579" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3}-{45}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{(\\frac{4}{3})}={270}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e99e15-33a7-50a8-b257-d2f54fa47ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250852} {:answer/id #uuid "3d59c05e-c0fa-5cfc-9ca6-d8e6cdd02580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251062} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2d0726-54ec-57ea-a54a-c02384eca19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251735} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8b0abc-64b9-5797-bc2a-b55cdc58fabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251903} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ee460a-830e-5d43-910b-fb39d4b81f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2d0726-54ec-57ea-a54a-c02384eca19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251735} :answer-field/id #uuid "e15068e8-bb8c-5630-a436-266e3b67d205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42337ba6-a7cb-5434-9483-5084bb012a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11762" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a2f289efe5b8f44c27b5d1ffe6d46d58a658f7f81155736288bc621909dd7727.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-6,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-6,6),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 36 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29405a5d-23a6-502b-be5d-56f1c497a84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{3}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250905} {:answer/id #uuid "718e38f3-a5c7-5905-95a6-c54405c63cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251484} {:answer/id #uuid "74a4bf91-0eba-544f-95ca-a4cafe2e4918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251514} {:answer/id #uuid "a800d469-ec72-5341-b219-06608ed25eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252001} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6e430e-c227-57e4-9f29-28cf38e29ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29405a5d-23a6-502b-be5d-56f1c497a84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{3}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250905} :answer-field/id #uuid "13384956-5f4c-5ced-a1de-1fad438fa14e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58d5c577-c726-5c8e-a1f0-5cd965b1b3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-117637" :question/problem "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$, the $y$-axis, and lines  $y=1$ and $y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{y}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2({e}^{3}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & =2({e}^{3}-e)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8eea0e-4cb9-5909-8a9c-73b546a569cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250812} {:answer/id #uuid "50846ea1-2a20-5022-b2c6-92023bf743ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251211} {:answer/id #uuid "c12cfbc4-0a87-57a5-b8bc-f3e3efb188bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252256} {:answer/id #uuid "d71160f7-9b73-5cde-ae0c-a48912324d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252461} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6802c-c089-5ea3-b944-d80f3ffb063f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6802c-c089-5ea3-b944-d80f3ffb063f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252689} :answer-field/id #uuid "95be207c-6257-5213-aca8-fa8e283f89d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "547a5328-8c82-5389-8e2c-39909fcd12b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-11764" :question/problem "![](images/83/83439b98fff25c021a2713448779167f25a0b9f04dd0d14b115a55f915e3501d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(12,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(12,6),$ we can substitute $x=12$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(12) \\\\ 36 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49b11be4-3658-52c8-8bf0-25fdceb40fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251165} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3ea915-9740-5a98-a33d-698d64c00a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251179} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4e7811-a45b-5e5c-98c0-b14ff8d1be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251199} {:answer/id #uuid "9d77d381-d721-506e-9675-3e44eb48d740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251912} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fb9a8c-18f6-586a-9cc9-426dc5df378c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49b11be4-3658-52c8-8bf0-25fdceb40fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251165} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ed92ee-7925-517a-a895-7f73c4059a69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50009c8b-7a58-5669-927f-169a6eff91b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117658" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9x+2\\pi }{3})+4.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9x+2\\pi }{3})+4=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=3,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c3c9aa-fed0-5631-b8da-f009c3d807d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251012} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbd6e53-ceba-56c7-abe2-a9d6ba652a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251206} {:answer/id #uuid "938c0634-0cbb-5e7e-b7d4-a95b9ff51bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251819} {:answer/id #uuid "a18d3f2c-bc11-502d-bf5d-b6b990acac82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251945} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fb0798-47ee-5cc7-9714-d66de9e94630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fbd6e53-ceba-56c7-abe2-a9d6ba652a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251206} :answer-field/id #uuid "bed4769b-f371-55d8-9550-13873ddaaded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a5543e1-6bbc-55cb-a040-aa25b0325f8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11766" :question/problem "![](images/67/6723e1c76d63216151e7c78d67c882ae98f9af950675e37ced726e8ce5aea4d3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-12,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-12,8),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{4}{3}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2430686f-6354-5ae3-a56a-86797016a196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250867} {:answer/id #uuid "84c30401-b464-5d98-8a9b-267f533cca26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251667} {:answer/id #uuid "97869399-0a57-5928-b446-b55279c01911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853} {:answer/id #uuid "a5606c09-671a-5dc4-abd2-15172f36984e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251979} {:answer/id #uuid "ab67f75e-df9d-5d97-89f6-287ab3d2483f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97869399-0a57-5928-b446-b55279c01911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f5b3662-7c4a-554a-a3fa-0abb3a6d9754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc8f3fd2-a7ad-59ae-81ee-947a33e17f52" :question/math-academy-id "q-117661" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4})=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=2,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1748716a-02da-5dc5-ad1d-869916cb8e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250758} {:answer/id #uuid "4658d1c7-fd54-59be-b2ad-96c992324dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251136} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5b2a35-69b6-5924-b604-4fc93e71c429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} 