we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ in the numerator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f785b1-458a-5fb7-aba5-77cefec577f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250579} {:answer/id #uuid "49bb700e-a1de-516d-87ba-f936ef57ceae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251166} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8f3721-64a5-5d00-9f26-2a8bc5dfc279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251428} {:answer/id #uuid "b44be054-75f2-50ef-84f8-a25b52eb4a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252138} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f36356-34e3-5f0e-a2e1-dfe997539867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02f785b1-458a-5fb7-aba5-77cefec577f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250579} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1fa67d6-78b4-56b2-a6da-1c23d4b075a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceef643d-5955-5dc4-9610-8fc14edd1b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120024" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})$ in the numerator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a688a79-b89b-5a35-859e-9149fddd7243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250780} {:answer/id #uuid "707de248-a804-5090-bb48-1024b7978c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251472} {:answer/id #uuid "88d27f46-cee8-5aaf-a22c-9714e10049d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251708} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7b26e1-8cd7-5f50-a6e4-a5249bb1359c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251750} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2cbc2a-146e-503b-a70d-071b522d1db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a688a79-b89b-5a35-859e-9149fddd7243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250780} :answer-field/id #uuid "245bab01-767a-51fa-a844-da9fd9dcf4a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a04b487-4a85-5ba7-af3b-ed0dfdddcb78" :question/math-academy-id "q-120032" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={105}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}) & =4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘})) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘})=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12fcc529-6327-5a47-a71b-9884c98ea937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250715} {:answer/id #uuid "452da317-cd5b-5e2c-a807-a5fb3eafe527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251127} {:answer/id #uuid "61086b08-503f-5afe-8d5f-93b7e8662a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251347} {:answer/id #uuid "b2646330-ca77-5a0d-b193-c06bcdc7a115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252111} {:answer/id #uuid "d26cc6ac-3efb-5846-8c19-760d22ea431d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61086b08-503f-5afe-8d5f-93b7e8662a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251347} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f04de3e-9c49-5ec2-bb16-a4b47af64121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad1365bd-d309-5764-8412-78c271945c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-120058" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04698c75-dc83-5fbb-8f53-9052909b1e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250585} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2c3035-0235-5603-a72a-eb385d8843e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250786} {:answer/id #uuid "6012bda1-9035-5aff-82de-2b534c046c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251340} {:answer/id #uuid "9aad87db-038e-549a-b557-3d3499e0f30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251882} {:answer/id #uuid "df46eab3-2fa6-5ef3-9539-002572011a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6012bda1-9035-5aff-82de-2b534c046c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251340} :answer-field/id #uuid "e90d146b-981f-56ed-88e6-d9ffffc3ab01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91b3ae38-a411-55e5-ba0c-b0976c1d82dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-120093" :question/problem "$8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{32}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{32})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) & =4[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) & =2[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})] \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee252b3-2fc9-58a1-a526-0ad834b9b6a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250686} {:answer/id #uuid "18391112-3f3a-57a1-9bfe-ef48a937491a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250766} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9cae62-9c68-5e87-9549-65a72091d03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250813} {:answer/id #uuid "405c4f12-0925-5899-8b0c-bf237460ec93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251088} {:answer/id #uuid "988c1baf-6f97-581c-b7f2-f8c57f43988e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988c1baf-6f97-581c-b7f2-f8c57f43988e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251863} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae027c21-9aa0-573c-bcf0-1d5cd80234b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03a455ba-2873-5368-831e-308d6a1a251d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12018" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-3.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-{u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x-3)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-(-3)\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3)+0 \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6f4f87-1ccd-589c-90e1-8890c55c15eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250932} {:answer/id #uuid "62a869bc-7ad8-5514-a78a-02dff1b40c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251359} {:answer/id #uuid "89286d84-c732-59b4-956d-104585bfdb9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251711} {:answer/id #uuid "94083696-43f4-54f2-b3f6-a3866a469f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251825} {:answer/id #uuid "a5579b86-e0b3-519e-954e-6b1008604303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5579b86-e0b3-519e-954e-6b1008604303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251978} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd003bc0-32be-5a18-932b-9a7f1e93dea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0eb4fa3e-52f2-52cc-a5b3-7f2b007d645a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120234" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049efc1d-4c7f-52f6-9cd8-9ae1c6f88cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,3.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250588} {:answer/id #uuid "66d1cb3d-aadc-5b70-9414-a02961598fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\text{ }\\,a,2.6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251397} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9dbdde-773a-5fb9-9921-474c631d48f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,2.7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252348} {:answer/id #uuid "cd82bd9e-d87e-5ee9-b28f-3cdf48082798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,2.8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252365} {:answer/id #uuid "d23227b1-bcd0-53d7-b1ec-28eb92816360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,4.6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca9dbdde-773a-5fb9-9921-474c631d48f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,2.7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252348} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6ee89f8-248f-5891-989b-af26eedc236e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c3da906-9678-5457-978c-c32d8842e209" :question/math-academy-id "q-120241" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-2a,a),$ where $a$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$  Round your answer to the nearest tenth where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2a)^{2}+{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{-2a})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})| \\\\  & ≈0.463\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -0.463\\,6 \\\\  & ≈2.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,2.7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191782ef-158c-5c61-8b5a-53889b26df66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250772} {:answer/id #uuid "27773dbc-82c6-5d97-8262-5945a24bc201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250891} {:answer/id #uuid "4683b3fe-eb8e-5220-9fa6-0a9afcd7c7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251140} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b8421d-9d4a-5d21-9ad8-60eec16fae69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252341} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fd4633-c96e-5bff-9e85-4b2076e4aa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4683b3fe-eb8e-5220-9fa6-0a9afcd7c7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251140} :answer-field/id #uuid "62588679-92ee-54c4-be84-9e822e9c62f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1855c488-31cc-5b55-93ba-bcc9b609f94f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120243" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-a,a),$ where $a$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-a)^{2}+{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{-a})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2075c86f-b918-575b-95e8-97859e70f90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253951} {:answer/id #uuid "52496fb6-06f9-54e1-953b-c03cfcd9334c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254387} {:answer/id #uuid "6724825c-031e-5303-abbe-a1eba30f6cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254603} {:answer/id #uuid "8470b6c3-3213-5f69-8af6-c90604195120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254848} {:answer/id #uuid "b04df24b-bdd0-5a86-9ea8-ddd583bd9c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52496fb6-06f9-54e1-953b-c03cfcd9334c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254387} :answer-field/id #uuid "5736a212-726e-5d37-950e-cb5984448244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cffd31fb-6e24-58d9-bedb-a4c483fcb431" :question/math-academy-id "q-120311" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,{225}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48fc86a1-f2ca-5fb6-98bb-2ba50587b6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254297} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e5dea-eb32-5ef7-b053-292457b74c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254647} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e86342-c856-59b9-acc7-8c16b2779f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255156} {:answer/id #uuid "bceb10bc-923e-5dff-8dfb-386a60cdb562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{2},6\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255360} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3e4f69-01dd-5d42-b6e1-f9b19a563b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48fc86a1-f2ca-5fb6-98bb-2ba50587b6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254297} :answer-field/id #uuid "af20ccec-9741-587e-8b2c-0fb701159ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fe83e25-a491-5f7c-b624-e2aecbbcfc65" :question/math-academy-id "q-120328" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $6,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${315}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=6,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61d023a1-2aa7-5888-bcf0-be644edf4473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254547} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2ff8db-4995-5531-9ef6-10f0585e85d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254923} {:answer/id #uuid "96f239ff-4f7d-598d-ae07-5b24ea0f2611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254999} {:answer/id #uuid "e192abdb-22ab-5b4e-8e0e-96f4a4dc0ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255671} {:answer/id #uuid "fed61b56-63c6-54aa-84e0-91cc6d252edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e192abdb-22ab-5b4e-8e0e-96f4a4dc0ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255671} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f82915f-10ef-56ab-829a-4cb0e79eacbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bb4ecb6-0b76-51e4-aa33-5be6f12e1dbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-120347" :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n2({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc04807-b343-515b-9c43-7382684abb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253811} {:answer/id #uuid "474a2bcd-a05b-55df-819f-946a0389240f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254275} {:answer/id #uuid "765c96ab-63cf-5369-9efe-a918084b0992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254714} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd5a3a3-2244-571c-a76f-0674c6cff271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254785} {:answer/id #uuid "9a878f24-4030-5bee-92fd-e58cdacc282e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "765c96ab-63cf-5369-9efe-a918084b0992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254714} :answer-field/id #uuid "38cdbaa8-adb7-5d1c-a1c4-ebe21d0fa1d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6564bb9b-39cf-5d47-ba17-b3f0f77c4c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120358" :question/problem "Express $3-6{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0778ee07-8e08-5767-b5b5-39fa9f5bd3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253726} {:answer/id #uuid "3141858b-3546-56b0-a225-f1d26fc10b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254099} {:answer/id #uuid "50e838be-c2e5-56cd-bd3f-42e1ef0167bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254372} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc93bfa-7e8d-5884-82ed-7bd2edacb355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254772} {:answer/id #uuid "e9177016-3d33-5bdf-b43c-ff255bcb6f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50e838be-c2e5-56cd-bd3f-42e1ef0167bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254372} :answer-field/id #uuid "32edd36e-c1e8-57b1-927a-22604b1325fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "908ae38c-0e5f-5da6-8253-8eb427ca6c40" :question/math-academy-id "q-120367" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a155516-d839-587a-bed9-42b9a2e072c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253747} {:answer/id #uuid "1129da34-d7d1-5e9f-8843-f5403e6bf93a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253820} {:answer/id #uuid "12c049a4-b3f5-57ad-8839-06ff45915613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253833} {:answer/id #uuid "4009cc1a-951f-501b-bdc2-6e69fb5a6357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254222} {:answer/id #uuid "a9834412-7636-54d2-8673-12769dbe9d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a155516-d839-587a-bed9-42b9a2e072c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253747} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d936aa-147e-55d6-93f2-fbed67c48196" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0e5e2dd-de81-57dc-8848-06ae6eac4cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-120455" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{12})^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=(12,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(6\\sqrt{3},-6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b5ead7-4261-5181-bae9-8edc851a01fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254116} {:answer/id #uuid "53c8fd3b-2fd0-5b9a-bac3-5049e7f07722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254400} {:answer/id #uuid "6f17da58-1b39-5035-8664-3b88056d92f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254656} {:answer/id #uuid "8be118cd-881d-5ff8-8a55-84e1e1df7e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254905} {:answer/id #uuid "95701b13-51f4-5588-a847-64a93ea10cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53c8fd3b-2fd0-5b9a-bac3-5049e7f07722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254400} :answer-field/id #uuid "76309cc7-4194-5ebe-adb7-704c2a92dd3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff4d5343-cd4d-538f-a96e-adc01ab24678" :question/math-academy-id "q-120456" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5}=-\\sqrt{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5} & =-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\sqrt{5})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})=2.034\\,443…≈2.034\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.034\\,4$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.034\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$\n\n![](images/f6/f659a13a5a4c9ae998c33b398b777decc8a5603fd18b5579ddebc897ea3dafca.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.107\\,2 \\\\  & =4.248\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.034\\,4\\cdot 4.248\\,7≈8.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}]
